        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: which letter arrives",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "word",
                "joined to ആണ്",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "word: എളുപ്പം. joined to ആണ്: എളുപ്പമാണ്, മ arrives.",
                "word: സുന്ദരം (sundaraṁ). joined to ആണ്: സുന്ദരമാണ്, മ arrives.",
                "word: കുട്ടി. joined to ആണ്: കുട്ടിയാണ്, യ arrives.",
                "word: ഭംഗി (bhaṅgi). joined to ആണ്: ഭംഗിയാണ്, യ arrives."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nothing is lost in any of the four. A letter arrives to carry the vowel of ആണ്, and which one depends on how the word ended — nothing else."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഇത് സുന്ദരമാണ്. — this is beautiful. ഇത് സുന്ദരം അല്ല (itŭ sundaraṁ alla). — this is not beautiful."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The second is not a second word. അല്ല stands apart, the way it has since you first met it, and സുന്ദരം (sundaraṁ) keeps its ം because nothing attached."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So one new quality gave you both ends of a line. That is how the far end of most qualities will arrive for a while — by denying the near one, which is both what you can build today and what a speaker often says anyway."
            }
          ]
        },
